        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.1,
            0.105,
            0.216,
            0.12
          ],
          "content": "POLYNOMIALS"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.873,
            0.104,
            0.898,
            0.119
          ],
          "content": "23"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.41,
            0.138,
            0.59,
            0.157
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "EXERCISE 2.2"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.125,
            0.168,
            0.898,
            0.252
          ],
          "content": "**1.** Find the zeroes of the following quadratic polynomials and verify the relationship between the zeroes and the coefficients. (i) $x ^ { 2 }   -   2 x   -   8$ (ii) $4 s ^ { 2 }   -   4 s   +   1$ (iii) $6 x ^ { 2 } - 3 - 7 x$ (iv) $4 u ^ { 2 }   + 8 u$ (v) $t ^ { 2 }   -   1 5$ (vi) $3 x ^ { 2 }   -   x   -   4$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.122,
            0.256,
            0.898,
            0.372
          ],
          "content": "**2.** Find a quadratic polynomial each with the given numbers as the sum and product of its zeroes respectively. 4 ${ \\frac { 1 } { 4 } } , - 1$ $- \\frac { 1 } { 4 } , \\frac { 1 } { 4 }$ ${ \\sqrt { 2 } } , { \\frac { 1 } { 3 } }$ $_ { 0 , \\sqrt { 5 } }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.391,
            0.251,
            0.411
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "2.4 Summary"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.418,
            0.575,
            0.436
          ],
          "content": "In this chapter, you have studied the following points:"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.124,
            0.441,
            0.899,
            0.477
          ],
          "content": "**1.** Polynomials of degrees 1, 2 and 3 are called linear, quadratic and cubic polynomials respectively."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.124,
            0.482,
            0.9,
            0.517
          ],
          "content": "**2.** A quadratic polynomial in x with real coefficients is of the form $ax^{2} + bx + c$ where a, b, c are real numbers with $a   \\neq   0$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.123,
            0.523,
            0.9,
            0.559
          ],
          "content": "**3.** The zeroes of a polynomial $p ( x )$ are precisely the x-coordinates of the points, where the graph of $y   =   p ( x )$ intersects the x - axis."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.124,
            0.564,
            0.9,
            0.599
          ],
          "content": "**4.** A quadratic polynomial can have at most 2 zeroes and a cubic polynomial can have at most 3 zeroes."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.124,
            0.604,
            0.765,
            0.622
          ],
          "content": "5. If   and   are the zeroes of the quadratic polynomial $a x ^ { 2 } + b x + c ,$ then"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.206,
            0.636,
            0.418,
            0.674
          ],
          "content": "\\alpha + \\beta = - \\frac{b}{a}, \\quad \\alpha \\beta = \\frac{c}{a}",
          "image_source": "images/page_13_equation_12.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.124,
            0.683,
            0.764,
            0.701
          ],
          "content": "6. If α, β, γ are the zeroes of the cubic polynomial $ax^{3} + bx^{2} + cx + d$ then"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.255,
            0.715,
            0.405,
            0.751
          ],
          "content": "\\alpha + \\beta + \\gamma = \\frac { - b } { a }",
          "image_source": "images/page_13_equation_14.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.255,
            0.773,
            0.432,
            0.805
          ],
          "content": "\\alpha \\beta + \\beta \\gamma + \\gamma \\alpha = \\frac { c } { a } ,",
          "image_source": "images/page_13_equation_15.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.175,
            0.824,
            0.366,
            0.86
          ],
          "content": "and $\\alpha \\beta \\gamma = { \\frac { - d } { a } }   .$"
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "bbox": [
            0.436,
            0.967,
            0.562,
            0.982
          ],
          "content": "Reprint 2026-27"
        }
      ]
    }
